1 OCCU Señor de Coquilla y de la Torre de Juan Vázquez, Corregidor de Segovia y de Jerez de la Frontera y Capitán General de la Frontera, prefecto de Granada.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_V%C3%A1zquez_de_Coronado (except where noted):
2 CONT
2 CONT born in Salamanca in Spain in 1523.
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to America when he was only 17 (1540?) and arrived first in Mexico. (http://genforum.genealogy.com/coronado/messages/29.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in settling Guatemala, and was mayor of the city of Santiago de Guatemala (today Antigua Guatemala), where he married Isabel Arias Dávila y Poblete in 1548.
2 CONT
2 CONT Went to El Salvador and was named mayor of San Salvador in 1549. Much later he appears to have occupied this same position in Honduras in 1556, then in Nicaragua in 1561, and finally in Costa Rica in 1562.  (http://genforum.geneal
2 CONC ogy.com/coronado/messages/29.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1562 he was named mayor of Nuevo Cartago y Costa Rica (which became Costa Rica in 1565)
2 CONT
2 CONT in 1565 he was named Adelantado of Costa Rica (Adelantados were Governor-like military office held by Spanish Conquistadores of the 16th and 17th centuries)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genforum.genealogy.com/coronado/messages/29.html: Went to Spain in 1565 where the king, Felipe II made him governor of Costa Rica and gave him the title of Adelantado (which was hereditary), but his boat was lost in a storm
2 CONC  off of the coast of Spain on his way back to the Americas.
2 CONT
2 CONT died in 1565. His title was inherited by his son, Gonzalo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia http://www.google.com/search?hl=en&q=Juan+Vazquez+de+Coronado+Anaya
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1562, Juan Vásquez de Coronado took the seat as governor of Veragua and found a group of Spaniards and Spanish mestizos living inland from the Pacific Coast. Throughout his exploration of the region, Coronado was more compassio
2 CONC nate in his treatment of the native people than were those who came before him. He found the highlands most suitable for settlement and moved his fellow Spaniards to the Cartago Valley. The climate here was milder and the soil ric
2 CONC h in nutrients from the lava of Volcán Irazú. The next year, Cartago became the capital of Costa Rica.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.vivacostarica.com/costa-rica-information/history-of-costa-rica-2.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelantado de Costa Rica.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_V%C3%A1zquez_de_Coronado
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Vásquez de Coronado y Anaya (Salamanca, 1523 – 1565) was a Spanish conquistador, remembered especially for his role in the colonization of Costa Rica, in Central America, where he gained a reputation for fairness, effective a
2 CONC dministration, and good relationships with the native population. He was a nephew of Francisco Vásquez de Coronado y Luján, who explored the southwestern United States between 1540 and 1542.
2 CONT
2 CONT By a decree given at Aranjuez by King Philip II of Spain on April 8, 1565, Juan Vásquez de Coronado was appointed as the first royal governor and first Adelantado (a hereditary title) of the province of Costa Rica, but his ship di
2 CONC sappeared in a storm off the coast of southern Spain while he was on his way to receive his appointments from the monarch. He was married to Isabel, a daughter of Pedro Arias Dávila, the principal conqueror of Panamá. The Vásquez 
2 CONC de Coronado Canton, one of the administrative divisions of the Province of San José, in the Republic Costa Rica, was named in his honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Vásquez de Coronado was born in Salamanca, Spain, the bastard son of a prominent nobleman, Gonzalo Vásquez de Coronado y Luján (c. 1500 – Valladolid, 1540), sixth Lord of Coquilla and la Torre de Juan Vásquez, perpetual Algua
2 CONC cil-Mayor of the Royal Audiencia (i.e., appellate court) of Valladolid, by an extramatrimonial relationship with Catalina de Anaya, from whom there are no further details but undoubtedly belonged to one of the principal families o
2 CONC f Salamanca.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vásquez de Coronado went very young to America, at only 17 years old, and established first in Mexico and then in Guatemala, where he started to fulfill official charges, such as Deputy of the Cabildo of the City of Santiago de lo
2 CONC s Caballeros and Ordinary Alcalde of Guatemala. In 1548 he married Isabel Arias-Dávila, the daughter of Captain Gaspar Arias-Dávila, and a first cousin to Pedrarias Dávila, both of whom were amongst the most prominent Conquerors o
2 CONC f New Spain and Guatemala. Having issue, he passed to El Salvador, where he enjoyed of an encomienda at Naolingo and was appointed Alcalde-Mayor of San Salvador in 1549. Later he appears occupying the same charge in Honduras in 15
2 CONC 56 and in Nicaragua in 1561 and finally in Costa Rica in 1562. In the country he distinguished himself for the pacific and fair way with which he executed the tasks of the conquest, being very respected and esteemed either by the 
2 CONC Indians and by the Spanish colonists. In 1565 he traveled to Spain where for his merits King Philip II granted him the charge of Governor of Costa Rica and the hereditary title of Adelantado of Costa Rica. However, in the return v
2 CONC oyage to take possession of his charges, his ship disappeared in a storm in front of the coasts of southern Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the most relevant characters of Costa Rican history is without doubt Juan Vásquez de Coronado, conqueror, first Governor and first Adelantado of Costa Rica. Despite his historical transcendency as one of the main builders o
2 CONC f the nation, Vásquez de Coronado is one of the main genearchs of the Costa Rican population, to such a point that a widespread study evidences that he is the progenitor of a true elite, that which until 1975 has given 29 of its 4
2 CONC 4 Heads of State, and more than two hundred Parliamentaries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife had at least one son, Gonzalo Vásquez de Coronado Arias, who married Ana Rodríguez del Padrón and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Diego Vásquez de Coronado Rodríguez, married to Francisca del Castillo Hoces, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gertrudis Vásquez de Coronado y del Castillo, married to Pedro Ocón y Trillo, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT María Ocón y Trillo Vásquez de Coronado, married to Diego Vásquez de Montiel, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Diego Vásquez Ocón y Trillo, married to Sebastiana Echavarría Navarro, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Micaela Montiel Echavarria, married to Simón Lacayo de Briones Pomar, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gabriel Lacayo Vásquez, married to Manuela Marenco Alarcón y Guerrero, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francisca Lacayo Marenco, married to Blas de la Cerda Aguilar, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Cayetano de la Cerda Lacayo, married to María de Jesús Taborga ..., and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Manuel Antonio de la Cerda Taborga
2 CONT
2 CONT Andrea Vásquez de Coronado Rodríguez, married to Diego Peláez de Lermos, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antonia Peláez Vásquez de Coronado, married to Portuguese Sebastián (Sebastião) Pereira Cardoso, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Pereira Peláez, married to José Sandoval Ocampo, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inés Sandoval Pereira, married to José Guevara Maldonado, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Alvaro Guevara Sandoval, married to María Sáenz Vásquez, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ángela Guevara Sáenz, married to Pedro Alvarado Vidamartel, and had: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro Alvarado Guevara, married to Manuela Baeza Baroto, and had: 
