2 CONT
2 CONT Álvaro IV de Bazán y Benavides, 2. marqués de SANTA CRUZ DE MUDELA, marqués del Viso, Grande de España de 2. clase, 2. señor de las villas de Valdepeñas y el Viso, gentilhombre de cámara del rey Felipe IV, cab (1584) y Trece OStg,
2 CONC  comendador de Alhambra y de la Solana, gobernador de Milán, maestre de campo general en Flandes, en el servicio de la Archiduquesa Isabel Clara Eugenia, del Consejo de Estado y Guerra, Mayordomo mayor de la reina Isabel de Borbón
2 CONC , * Nápoles 12.9.1571, + 1646;
2 CONT = x Almagro 1590 Guiomar Manrique de Lara, hija de Bernardino Manrique de Lara, comendador de Herrera, OCal, hijo de los 2. duques de Nájera, y de Ana de Castro, hermana de Antonio de Castro, alcaide de la fortaleza de Bolaños, lu
2 CONC gar de la OCal, e hija de Hernando de Castro, alcaide de la misma fortaleza, y de Leonor de Guelva, hija de Hernando de Guelva y de María Rodríguez
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81lvaro_de_Baz%C3%A1n,_2nd_Marquis_of_Santa_Cruz
2 CONT
2 CONT Álvaro de Bazán, 2nd Marquis of Santa Cruz, a.k.a. Álvaro de Bazán y Benavides, a.k.a. Alvaro II de Bazán, (Naples, Italy, 12 September 1571 – 1646), was the son of Álvaro de Bazán, 1st Marquis of Santa Cruz.
2 CONT
2 CONT  He was Governor of the Duchy of Milan (1630–1631), Maestro de Campo General in Flanders under the Governess of the Spanish Netherlands Isabel Clara Eugenia (1631), a Spanish Navy commander and Army land General during the Thirty 
2 CONC Years War, Member of the Spanish Councils of War and the State Council, Marquis of El Viso, Lord of Valdepeñas, and a Grandee of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Älvaro II main military actions were:
2 CONT
2 CONT  1596 Defense of Cádiz against the attack of the English Fleet sent by Queen Elizabeth I of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1603 Captain General of the Galleys of the kingdom of Naples.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1604 Naval actions in the Mediterranean African Coast, Longo Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1605 Siege and Conquest of Estarcho and the City of Durazzo, on the Albanian Coast
2 CONT
2 CONT  1612 In May, commanding the Naples kingdom Fleet of Galleys, with Ottavio d'Aragona commanding those of the kingdom of Sicily, sink several galleys from Algiers.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1614 Take part in the transport to North Africa of morisco´s from Spain not willing to become Roman Catholics while living in mainland Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1622 Takes part in an African expedition.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1625 Relieved Genoa under siege from the France and the Duchy of Savoy. His action was immortalized in the painting Relief of Genoa by the Marquis of Santa Cruz (1634) by Antonio de Pereda
2 CONT
2 CONT  1629 Commander of the Mediterranean Galleys.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1630–1631 Appointed Governor of the Duchy of Milan on the death of Ambrosio Spinola.
2 CONT
2 CONT  1631 General Governor of the Army of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married in 1590, in Almagro, Spain, to Guiomar Manrique de Lara. Their descendents included two sons and five daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT The evolution of the title since the middle of the XVII Century
2 CONT
2 CONT  Mauro Alvaro, the eldest male, was 3rd Marquis, marrying on 22 October 1627, Genoese noble woman Maria Francesca Doria, a daughter of Carlo I Doria del Carretto, 1st Duke of Tursi, a Grandee of Spain with many other lesser titles
2 CONC , (Genoa, Italy, 15 October 1576 – Genoa, Italy, 19 December 1649) and Placidia Spinola, (1584–1660), 2nd Marchioness of Calice and Veppo, between other titles. No surviving adult issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT  The 4th Marchioness, was the eldest sister of the 2nd Marquis Alvaro II, named Maria Eugenia de Bazán y Benavides. She was married in 1620 to Jerónimo Pimentel, Marquis of Bayona, Viceroy of Sardinia, 1626–1631, deceased 15 April
2 CONC  1631, with issue. He was the 8th son of Juan Alfonso Pimentel de Herrera, 5rh Duke of Benavente, deceased 7 November 1621, Viceroy of Valencia, 1598–1602, Viceroy of Naples, 1603–1610. The other male of the family, Fernando, beca
2 CONC me Chancellor, Rector, of the University of Salamanca, and later, after ecclesiastical jobs at Seville and Cordoba, Archbishop of Palermo, Sicily, Italy .
2 CONT
2 CONT  Thus, the title of Marquis of Bazán became attached to a branch of the Pimentel family, a female called Mencia Pimentel y Bazan.When she married a "Benavides" male, it was agreed by the couple that if they had a male, the male ch
2 CONC ild would be named Francisco Diego de Bazan y Benavides to honor her ancestors and "recuperate" her ancestors name, something not uncommon between the High Spanish Nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In fact, a son was born and named Francisco de Bazan y Benavides (died in 1680). He was Captain General of the Spanish galleys and Viceroy of Sicily, (1674 Interim), using always his mother's ancestors' family name, "Bazán", as a
2 CONC  token of respect, and possibly, duty.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%81lvaro_de_Baz%C3%A1n,_2nd_Marquis_of_Santa_Cruz
